#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167717} :activity/id #uuid "1bdab585-5331-596a-b7b4-0a1f2915ca75" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049562 :topic/id #uuid "ffe9e721-b26e-55d0-9e1f-e1d2a56a5a72" :topic/math-academy-id 3179} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168103 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a differential equation of the form \n\n$$\ny ' = f ( a x + b y ),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are real constants. In this case, we can rewrite the equation as a separable differential equation using the substitution $z= ax+by.$ We then proceed to solve this separable equation.\n\n To see how we apply this method, let's consider the following example: \n\n$$\ny' = (x+y)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = g(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z^2+ 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡z & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the general solution to the equation in terms of $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\arctan {(x+y)}  = x + C\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f67d9540-b344-56cf-9d27-747a656e8f6d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5345 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2d610c8e-cba3-5dd1-81e3-dc49d08dbbe3" :step/math-academy-id 33447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166701 :question/id #uuid "870077e3-99f9-52b9-ac39-26bbc3827466" :question/math-academy-id "e-9035"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483412}] :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"} :step/id #uuid "d30e0cc8-f5fd-57d0-abd3-963f38723c6f" :step/math-academy-id 33752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166702 :question/id #uuid "d3145755-b414-57e5-8039-04ceb1a3a5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9037"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380511} {:db/id #